{"traj_id": "deepmath-104190_0", "problem": "Given the polynomial \\((1-z)^{b_{1}}(1-z^{2})^{b_{2}}(1-z^{3})^{b_{3}} \\cdots(1-z^{32})^{b_{32}}\\), where \\( b_{i} \\) are positive integers for \\( i = 1, 2, \\ldots, 32 \\), and this polynomial has the property that after expanding it and removing the terms with \\( z \\) raised to powers higher than 32, what remains is \\( 1 - 2z \\). Determine \\( b_{32} \\), and express your answer as the difference of two powers of 2.", "gold": "2^{27} - 2^{11}", "pred": "2^{27}-2^{11}", "is_correct": true, "tier": NaN, "n_tokens": 9814, "meta_leak": [], "paragraphs": ["Let me work through this problem carefully.\n\nWe have the polynomial:\n$$P(z) = (1-z)^{b_1}(1-z^2)^{b_2}(1-z^3)^{b_3} \\cdots (1-z^{32})^{b_{32}}$$", "When we expand this and keep only terms up to $z^{32}$, we get:\n$$P(z) \\mod z^{33} = 1 - 2z$$", "This means the coefficient of $z^0$ is 1, the coefficient of $z^1$ is $-2$, and the coefficient of $z^k$ is 0 for $k = 2, 3, \\ldots, 32$.", "Let me think about this. The product of $(1 - z^i)$ for $i = 1, 2, \\ldots, 32$ is related to the partition function generating function.", "Actually, let me think about this differently. We know that:\n$$\\prod_{i=1}^{\\infty} (1 - z^i) = \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} (-1)^n z^{n(3n-1)/2}$$", "by Euler's pentagonal number theorem. The pentagonal numbers are $n(3n-1)/2$ for $n = 0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\ldots$, which gives: $0, 1, 2, 5, 7, 12, 15, 22, 26, \\ldots$", "Wait, let me recalculate:\n- $n = 0$: $0$\n- $n = 1$: $1 \\cdot 2/2 = 1$\n- $n = -1$: $(-1)(-4)/2 = 2$\n- $n = 2$: $2 \\cdot 5/2 = 5$\n- $n = -2$: $(-2)(-7)/2 = 7$\n- $n = 3$: $3 \\cdot 8/2 = 12$\n- $n = -3$: $(-3)(-10)/2 = 15$\n- $n = 4$: $4 \\cdot 11/2 = 22$\n- $n = -4$: $(-4)(-13)/2 = 26$\n- $n = 5$: $5 \\cdot 14/2 = 35$\n- $n = -5$: $(-5)(-16)/2 = 40$", "So $\\prod_{i=1}^{\\infty} (1 - z^i) = 1 - z - z^2 + z^5 + z^7 - z^{12} - z^{15} + z^{22} + z^{26} - z^{35} - \\cdots$", "Now, our polynomial is:\n$$P(z) = \\prod_{i=1}^{32} (1 - z^i)^{b_i}$$", "If all $b_i = 1$, then:\n$$\\prod_{i=1}^{32} (1 - z^i) = \\prod_{i=1}^{\\infty} (1 - z^i) \\mod z^{33}$$", "Wait, actually $(1-z^{33})$ and higher terms don't affect terms up to $z^{32}$. So:\n$$\\prod_{i=1}^{32} (1 - z^i) \\equiv \\prod_{i=1}^{\\infty} (1 - z^i) \\mod z^{33}$$", "Hmm, wait. That's not quite right either. $\\prod_{i=1}^{32} (1-z^i)$ is NOT the same as $\\prod_{i=1}^{\\infty} (1-z^i)$ modulo $z^{33}$. Because $\\prod_{i=1}^{\\infty} (1-z^i) = \\prod_{i=1}^{32}(1-z^i) \\cdot \\prod_{i=33}^{\\infty}(1-z^i)$, and $\\prod_{i=33}^{\\infty}(1-z^i) = 1 + O(z^{33})$ since each factor is $1 + O(z^{33})$. So yes, $\\prod_{i=1}^{32}(1-z^i) \\equiv \\prod_{i=1}^{\\infty}(1-z^i) \\mod z^{33}$.", "So $\\prod_{i=1}^{32}(1-z^i) \\equiv 1 - z - z^2 + z^5 + z^7 - z^{12} - z^{15} + z^{22} + z^{26} \\mod z^{33}$", "But we want $\\prod_{i=1}^{32}(1-z^i)^{b_i} \\equiv 1 - 2z \\mod z^{33}$.", "So the ratio is:\n$$\\frac{\\prod_{i=1}^{32}(1-z^i)^{b_i}}{\\prod_{i=1}^{32}(1-z^i)} \\equiv \\frac{1-2z}{1 - z - z^2 + z^5 + z^7 - z^{12} - z^{15} + z^{22} + z^{26}} \\mod z^{33}$$", "Let me define $c_i = b_i - 1$ for $i = 1, \\ldots, 32$. Then:\n$$\\prod_{i=1}^{32}(1-z^i)^{c_i} \\equiv \\frac{1-2z}{1 - z - z^2 + z^5 + z^7 - z^{12} - z^{15} + z^{22} + z^{26}} \\mod z^{33}$$", "This seems complicated. Let me think of a different approach.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe I should think about this problem using the properties of the partition function.", "We know that $\\frac{1}{\\prod_{i=1}^{\\infty}(1-z^i)} = \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} p(n) z^n$ where $p(n)$ is the partition number.", "And $\\prod_{i=1}^{\\infty}(1-z^i) = \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} (-1)^n z^{n(3n-1)/2}$", "Hmm, let me try yet another approach.\n\nLet me write $P(z) = \\prod_{i=1}^{32} (1-z^i)^{b_i}$.", "Taking the logarithm (formal power series):\n$$\\log P(z) = \\sum_{i=1}^{32} b_i \\log(1-z^i) = \\sum_{i=1}^{32} b_i \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{k+1} z^{ik}}{k}$$", "$$= \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{k+1}}{k} \\sum_{i=1}^{32} b_i z^{ik}$$", "Now, $P(z) \\equiv 1 - 2z \\mod z^{33}$, so $\\log P(z) \\equiv \\log(1-2z) \\mod z^{33}$ (well, we need to be careful, but since $P(z) = 1 + O(z)$, the logarithm is well-defined).", "$\\log(1-2z) = -2z - 2^2 z^2/2 - 2^3 z^3/3 - \\cdots = \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{k+1}(-2)^k z^k}{k}$", "Wait, $\\log(1-x) = -\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} x^k/k$. So $\\log(1-2z) = -\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} (2z)^k/k = \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-2)^k}{k} z^k$.", "And $\\log P(z) = \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{k+1}}{k} \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{32} b_i z^{ik}\\right)$", "$= \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{k+1}}{k} \\sum_{i: ik \\leq 32} b_i z^{ik}$", "Wait, I need to be more careful. The inner sum $\\sum_{i=1}^{32} b_i z^{ik}$ - when we expand this as a power series in $z$, the term $z^{ik}$ has degree $ik$. So for the coefficient of $z^n$ in $\\log P(z)$, we need $ik = n$, i.e., $i = n/k$ where $k$ divides $n$.", "So the coefficient of $z^n$ in $\\log P(z)$ is:\n$$\\sum_{k | n, k \\leq 32} \\frac{(-1)^{k+1}}{k} b_{n/k}$$", "where $b_j = 0$ if $j > 32$.", "Wait, let me redo this. $\\log P(z) = \\sum_{i=1}^{32} b_i \\log(1-z^i) = \\sum_{i=1}^{32} b_i \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{k+1} (z^i)^k}{k}$", "The coefficient of $z^n$ is $\\sum_{i=1}^{32} b_i \\cdot \\frac{(-1)^{k+1}}{k}$ where $k = n/i$ (and $k$ must be a positive integer, i.e., $i | n$).", "So the coefficient of $z^n$ in $\\log P(z)$ is $\\sum_{i | n, i \\leq 32} b_i \\frac{(-1)^{n/i + 1}}{n/i}$", "$= \\sum_{i | n, i \\leq 32} b_i \\frac{(-1)^{n/i + 1} \\cdot i}{n}$", "Now, $\\log(1-2z) = -\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(2z)^k}{k}$, so the coefficient of $z^n$ is $-\\frac{2^n}{n}$.", "So we need:\n$$\\sum_{i | n, i \\leq 32} b_i \\frac{(-1)^{n/i + 1} \\cdot i}{n} = -\\frac{2^n}{n}$$", "for $n = 1, 2, \\ldots, 32$.", "This simplifies to:\n$$\\sum_{i | n, i \\leq 32} b_i (-1)^{n/i + 1} \\cdot i = -2^n$$", "for $n = 1, 2, \\ldots, 32$.", "Let me define $a_n = \\sum_{i | n, i \\leq 32} b_i (-1)^{n/i + 1} \\cdot i$ for $n \\leq 32$.", "We need $a_n = -2^n$ for $n = 1, 2, \\ldots, 32$.", "For $n = 1$: $b_1 \\cdot (-1)^2 \\cdot 1 = b_1 = -2$", "But $b_1$ is a positive integer! Contradiction?", "Wait, let me recheck. $(-1)^{n/i+1}$ for $n = 1, i = 1$: $(-1)^{1+1} = (-1)^2 = 1$. So $a_1 = b_1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 = b_1$.", "And we need $a_1 = -2^1 = -2$. So $b_1 = -2$.", "But $b_1$ is supposed to be a positive integer. This is a problem.", "Hmm, let me re-examine the problem. Maybe I'm making a sign error.", "$\\log(1-z^i) = -\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{z^{ik}}{k}$", "So $\\log P(z) = \\sum_{i=1}^{32} b_i \\left(-\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{z^{ik}}{k}\\right) = -\\sum_{i=1}^{32} b_i \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{z^{ik}}{k}$", "The coefficient of $z^n$ in $\\log P(z)$ is $-\\sum_{i | n, i \\leq 32} \\frac{b_i}{n/i} = -\\sum_{i | n, i \\leq 32} \\frac{b_i \\cdot i}{n}$", "And the coefficient of $z^n$ in $\\log(1-2z)$ is $-\\frac{2^n}{n}$.", "So: $-\\sum_{i | n, i \\leq 32} \\frac{b_i \\cdot i}{n} = -\\frac{2^n}{n}$", "$\\sum_{i | n, i \\leq 32} b_i \\cdot i = 2^n$", "for $n = 1, 2, \\ldots, 32$.", "For $n = 1$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 = 2$, so $b_1 = 2$. ✓", "For $n = 2$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 = 4$, so $2 + 2b_2 = 4$, $b_2 = 1$. ✓", "For $n = 3$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_3 \\cdot 3 = 8$, so $2 + 3b_3 = 8$, $b_3 = 2$. ✓", "For $n = 4$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_4 \\cdot 4 = 16$, so $2 + 2 + 4b_4 = 16$, $b_4 = 3$. ✓", "For $n = 5$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_5 \\cdot 5 = 32$, so $2 + 5b_5 = 32$, $b_5 = 6$. ✓", "For $n = 6$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_3 \\cdot 3 + b_6 \\cdot 6 = 64$, so $2 + 2 + 6 + 6b_6 = 64$, $10 + 6b_6 = 64$, $b_6 = 9$. ✓", "For $n = 7$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_7 \\cdot 7 = 128$, so $2 + 7b_7 = 128$, $b_7 = 18$. ✓", "For $n = 8$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_4 \\cdot 4 + b_8 \\cdot 8 = 256$, so $2 + 2 + 12 + 8b_8 = 256$, $16 + 8b_8 = 256$, $b_8 = 30$. ✓", "For $n = 9$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_3 \\cdot 3 + b_9 \\cdot 9 = 512$, so $2 + 6 + 9b_9 = 512$, $8 + 9b_9 = 512$, $b_9 = 56$. ✓", "For $n = 10$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_5 \\cdot 5 + b_{10} \\cdot 10 = 1024$, so $2 + 2 + 30 + 10b_{10} = 1024$, $34 + 10b_{10} = 1024$, $b_{10} = 99$. ✓", "For $n = 11$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_{11} \\cdot 11 = 2048$, so $2 + 11b_{11} = 2048$, $b_{11} = 186$. ✓", "For $n = 12$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_3 \\cdot 3 + b_4 \\cdot 4 + b_6 \\cdot 6 + b_{12} \\cdot 12 = 4096$", "$2 + 2 + 6 + 12 + 54 + 12b_{12} = 4096$", "$76 + 12b_{12} = 4096$", "$12b_{12} = 4020$", "$b_{12} = 335$. ✓\n\nFor $n = 13$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_{13} \\cdot 13 = 8192$, so $2 + 13b_{13} = 8192$, $b_{13} = 630$. ✓", "For $n = 14$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_7 \\cdot 7 + b_{14} \\cdot 14 = 16384$", "$2 + 2 + 126 + 14b_{14} = 16384$", "$130 + 14b_{14} = 16384$", "$14b_{14} = 16254$", "$b_{14} = 1161$. ✓\n\nFor $n = 15$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_3 \\cdot 3 + b_5 \\cdot 5 + b_{15} \\cdot 15 = 32768$", "$2 + 6 + 30 + 15b_{15} = 32768$", "$38 + 15b_{15} = 32768$", "$15b_{15} = 32730$", "$b_{15} = 2182$. ✓\n\nFor $n = 16$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_4 \\cdot 4 + b_8 \\cdot 8 + b_{16} \\cdot 16 = 65536$", "$2 + 2 + 12 + 240 + 16b_{16} = 65536$", "$256 + 16b_{16} = 65536$", "$16b_{16} = 65280$", "$b_{16} = 4080$. ✓\n\nFor $n = 17$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_{17} \\cdot 17 = 131072$, so $2 + 17b_{17} = 131072$, $b_{17} = 7708$. ✓", "For $n = 18$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_3 \\cdot 3 + b_6 \\cdot 6 + b_9 \\cdot 9 + b_{18} \\cdot 18 = 262144$", "$2 + 2 + 6 + 54 + 504 + 18b_{18} = 262144$", "$568 + 18b_{18} = 262144$", "$18b_{18} = 261576$", "$b_{18} = 14532$. ✓", "For $n = 19$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_{19} \\cdot 19 = 524288$, so $2 + 19b_{19} = 524288$, $b_{19} = 27594$. ✓", "For $n = 20$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_4 \\cdot 4 + b_5 \\cdot 5 + b_{10} \\cdot 10 + b_{20} \\cdot 20 = 1048576$", "$2 + 2 + 12 + 30 + 990 + 20b_{20} = 1048576$", "$1036 + 20b_{20} = 1048576$", "$20b_{20} = 1047540$", "$b_{20} = 52377$. ✓", "For $n = 21$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_3 \\cdot 3 + b_7 \\cdot 7 + b_{21} \\cdot 21 = 2097152$", "$2 + 6 + 126 + 21b_{21} = 2097152$", "$134 + 21b_{21} = 2097152$", "$21b_{21} = 2097018$", "$b_{21} = 99858$. ✓", "For $n = 22$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_{11} \\cdot 11 + b_{22} \\cdot 22 = 4194304$", "$2 + 2 + 2046 + 22b_{22} = 4194304$", "$2050 + 22b_{22} = 4194304$", "$22b_{22} = 4192254$", "$b_{22} = 190557$. ✓", "For $n = 23$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_{23} \\cdot 23 = 8388608$, so $2 + 23b_{23} = 8388608$, $b_{23} = 364722$. ✓", "For $n = 24$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_3 \\cdot 3 + b_4 \\cdot 4 + b_6 \\cdot 6 + b_8 \\cdot 8 + b_{12} \\cdot 12 + b_{24} \\cdot 24 = 16777216$", "$2 + 2 + 6 + 12 + 54 + 240 + 4020 + 24b_{24} = 16777216$", "$4336 + 24b_{24} = 16777216$", "$24b_{24} = 16772880$", "$b_{24} = 698870$. ✓", "For $n = 25$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_5 \\cdot 5 + b_{25} \\cdot 25 = 33554432$", "$2 + 30 + 25b_{25} = 33554432$", "$32 + 25b_{25} = 33554432$", "$25b_{25} = 33554400$", "$b_{25} = 1342176$. ✓", "For $n = 26$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_{13} \\cdot 13 + b_{26} \\cdot 26 = 67108864$", "$2 + 2 + 8190 + 26b_{26} = 67108864$", "$8194 + 26b_{26} = 67108864$", "$26b_{26} = 67099670$", "Hmm, let me check: $67108864 - 8194 = 67100670$. $67100670/26 = 2580795$. Let me verify: $26 \\times 2580795 = 67100670$. $26 \\times 2500000 = 65000000$, $26 \\times 80795 = 2100670$. $65000000 + 2100670 = 67100670$. ✓", "So $b_{26} = 2580795$. ✓", "For $n = 27$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_3 \\cdot 3 + b_9 \\cdot 9 + b_{27} \\cdot 27 = 134217728$", "$2 + 6 + 504 + 27b_{27} = 134217728$", "$512 + 27b_{27} = 134217728$", "$27b_{27} = 134217216$", "$b_{27} = 4970971$? Let me check: $134217216/27$. $27 \\times 4000000 = 108000000$. $134217216 - 108000000 = 26217216$. $27 \\times 900000 = 24300000$. $26217216 - 24300000 = 1917216$. $27 \\times 70000 = 1890000$. $1917216 - 1890000 = 27216$. $27 \\times 1000 = 27000$. $27216 - 27000 = 216$. $27 \\times 8 = 216$. So $b_{27} = 4000000 + 900000 + 70000 + 1000 + 8 = 4971008$.", "Let me verify: $27 \\times 4971008 = 27 \\times 4000000 + 27 \\times 900000 + 27 \\times 70000 + 27 \\times 1000 + 27 \\times 8 = 108000000 + 24300000 + 1890000 + 27000 + 216 = 134217216$. ✓", "For $n = 28$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_4 \\cdot 4 + b_7 \\cdot 7 + b_{14} \\cdot 14 + b_{28} \\cdot 28 = 268435456$", "$2 + 2 + 12 + 126 + 16254 + 28b_{28} = 268435456$", "$16496 + 28b_{28} = 268435456$", "$28b_{28} = 268418960$", "$b_{28} = 9586391$? Let me check: $268418960/28$. $28 \\times 9000000 = 252000000$. $268418960 - 252000000 = 16418960$. $28 \\times 500000 = 14000000$. $16418960 - 14000000 = 2418960$. $28 \\times 80000 = 2240000$. $2418960 - 2240000 = 178960$. $28 \\times 6000 = 168000$. $178960 - 168000 = 10960$. $28 \\times 391 = 10948$. $10960 - 10948 = 12$. That doesn't divide evenly.", "Let me recheck. $2 + 2 + 12 + 126 + 16254 = 16396$. ", "$268435456 - 16396 = 268419060$. $268419060/28 = 9586395$. Let me verify: $28 \\times 9586395 = 28 \\times 9000000 + 28 \\times 586395 = 252000000 + 16419060 = 268419060$. ✓", "So $b_{28} = 9586395$.", "For $n = 29$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_{29} \\cdot 29 = 536870912$, so $2 + 29b_{29} = 536870912$, $b_{29} = 18512790$. ✓", "For $n = 30$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_3 \\cdot 3 + b_5 \\cdot 5 + b_6 \\cdot 6 + b_{10} \\cdot 10 + b_{15} \\cdot 15 + b_{30} \\cdot 30 = 1073741824$", "$2 + 2 + 6 + 30 + 54 + 990 + 32730 + 30b_{30} = 1073741824$", "$33814 + 30b_{30} = 1073741824$", "$30b_{30} = 1073708010$", "$b_{30} = 35790267$. ✓", "For $n = 31$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_{31} \\cdot 31 = 2147483648$, so $2 + 31b_{31} = 2147483648$, $b_{31} = 69273666$. ✓", "For $n = 32$: $b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_4 \\cdot 4 + b_8 \\cdot 8 + b_{16} \\cdot 16 + b_{32} \\cdot 32 = 4294967296$", "$2 + 2 + 12 + 240 + 65280 + 32b_{32} = 4294967296$", "$65536 + 32b_{32} = 4294967296$", "$32b_{32} = 4294901760$", "$b_{32} = 134215680$.", "Let me verify: $32 \\times 134215680 = 4294901760$. $32 \\times 134000000 = 4288000000$. $32 \\times 215680 = 6901760$. $4288000000 + 6901760 = 4294901760$. ✓", "So $b_{32} = 134215680$.", "Now I need to express this as a difference of two powers of 2.", "$134215680$. Let me see. $2^{27} = 134217728$. $2^{26} = 67108864$.", "$134217728 - 134215680 = 2048 = 2^{11}$.", "So $b_{32} = 2^{27} - 2^{11}$.", "Let me verify: $2^{27} = 134217728$, $2^{11} = 2048$. $134217728 - 2048 = 134215680$. ✓", "So $b_{32} = 2^{27} - 2^{11}$.", "Wait, let me double-check my computation for $n = 32$.", "The divisors of 32 that are $\\leq 32$ are: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32.", "$\\sum_{i | 32} b_i \\cdot i = b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_4 \\cdot 4 + b_8 \\cdot 8 + b_{16} \\cdot 16 + b_{32} \\cdot 32$", "$= 2 \\times 1 + 1 \\times 2 + 3 \\times 4 + 30 \\times 8 + 4080 \\times 16 + b_{32} \\times 32$", "$= 2 + 2 + 12 + 240 + 65280 + 32b_{32}$", "$= 65536 + 32b_{32}$", "We need this to equal $2^{32} = 4294967296$.", "$32b_{32} = 4294967296 - 65536 = 4294901760$", "$b_{32} = 4294901760/32 = 134215680$", "And $134215680 = 2^{27} - 2^{11}$.", "Actually, let me verify this more carefully.\n\n$2^{27} = 134217728$\n$2^{11} = 2048$\n$2^{27} - 2^{11} = 134217728 - 2048 = 134215680$ ✓", "Great, so the answer is $2^{27} - 2^{11}$."], "answer_text": "Looking at this problem, I need to find $b_{32}$ where the product $\\prod_{i=1}^{32}(1-z^i)^{b_i} \\equiv 1-2z \\pmod{z^{33}}$.\n\n## Setting Up the Logarithmic Approach\n\nSince $P(z) = 1 + O(z)$, I can work with the formal logarithm. Taking $\\log$ of both sides:\n\n$$\\log P(z) = \\sum_{i=1}^{32} b_i \\log(1-z^i) = \\sum_{i=1}^{32} b_i \\left(-\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{z^{ik}}{k}\\right)$$\n\nThe coefficient of $z^n$ in $\\log P(z)$ is $-\\sum_{i \\mid n,\\, i\\leq 32}\\frac{b_i \\cdot i}{n}$.\n\nThe coefficient of $z^n$ in $\\log(1-2z) = -\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(2z)^k}{k}$ is $-\\frac{2^n}{n}$.\n\n**Equating coefficients** for each $n \\leq 32$:\n$$\\boxed{\\sum_{i \\mid n,\\, i \\leq 32} b_i \\cdot i = 2^n}$$\n\n## Computing the $b_i$ Values\n\nUsing Möbius-inversion-style recursion, I compute each $b_n$:\n\n| $n$ | Divisors $\\leq 32$ | Sum of $b_i \\cdot i$ for proper divisors | $b_n$ |\n|-----|---|---|---|\n| 1 | {1} | 0 | $2/1 = 2$ |\n| 2 | {1,2} | 2 | $(4-2)/2 = 1$ |\n| 3 | {1,3} | 2 | $(8-2)/3 = 2$ |\n| 4 | {1,2,4} | 4 | $(16-4)/4 = 3$ |\n| 5 | {1,5} | 2 | $(32-2)/5 = 6$ |\n| 6 | {1,2,3,6} | 10 | $(64-10)/6 = 9$ |\n| 7 | {1,7} | 2 | $(128-2)/7 = 18$ |\n| 8 | {1,2,4,8} | 16 | $(256-16)/8 = 30$ |\n| 12 | {1,2,3,4,6,12} | 76 | $(4096-76)/12 = 335$ |\n| 16 | {1,2,4,8,16} | 256 | $(65536-256)/16 = 4080$ |\n| 32 | {1,2,4,8,16,32} | ? | ? |\n\n## Computing $b_{32}$\n\nThe divisors of 32 (all $\\leq 32$) are $\\{1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32\\}$:\n\n$$\\sum_{i\\mid 32} b_i \\cdot i = b_1 \\cdot 1 + b_2 \\cdot 2 + b_4 \\cdot 4 + b_8 \\cdot 8 + b_{16}\\cdot 16 + b_{32}\\cdot 32$$\n\nSubstituting known values:\n$$= 2(1) + 1(2) + 3(4) + 30(8) + 4080(16) + 32\\,b_{32}$$\n$$= 2 + 2 + 12 + 240 + 65280 + 32\\,b_{32} = 65536 + 32\\,b_{32}$$\n\nSetting equal to $2^{32}$:\n$$65536 + 32\\,b_{32} = 4\\,294\\,967\\,296$$\n$$32\\,b_{32} = 4\\,294\\,901\\,760$$\n$$b_{32} = 134\\,215\\,680$$\n\n## Expressing as a Difference of Powers of 2\n\nNoting that $65536 = 2^{16}$ and $2^{32}/32 = 2^{27}$:\n\n$$b_{32} = \\frac{2^{32} - 2^{16}}{2^5} = 2^{27} - 2^{11}$$\n\n**Verification:** $2^{27} = 134\\,217\\,728$ and $2^{11} = 2\\,048$, so $134\\,217\\,728 - 2\\,048 = 134\\,215\\,680$ ✓\n\n$$\\boxed{2^{27}-2^{11}}$$", "annotations": {"27B·v4": {"nodes": [{"node_id": 0, "p_start": 0, "p_end": 2, "label": "SU", "deps": [], "branch": 0, "path": "M", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 1, "p_start": 3, "p_end": 3, "label": "EX", "deps": [0], "branch": 1, "path": "D", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 2, "p_start": 4, "p_end": 5, "label": "RC", "deps": [1], "branch": 1, "path": "D", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 3, "p_start": 6, "p_end": 7, "label": "CP", "deps": [2], "branch": 1, "path": "D", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 4, "p_start": 8, "p_end": 8, "label": "SU", "d